REAL FIX: stop libtool's BUILD() from trying to re-run autotools it does not have.

Symptom, first form -- the build dies almost immediately with

    CDPATH="${ZSH_VERSION+.}:" && cd . && /bin/sh 'build-aux/missing' aclocal-1.17 -I m4
    build-aux/missing: line 85: aclocal-1.17: command not found
    WARNING: 'aclocal-1.17' is missing on your system.
    make: *** [Makefile:1069: aclocal.m4] Error 127

The missing command is NOT `aclocal`. It is `aclocal-1.17`, the *version
suffixed* name. Makefile:447,456 say

    ACLOCAL = ${SHELL} '/sources/libtool-2.5.4/build-aux/missing' aclocal-1.17
    AUTOMAKE = ${SHELL} '/sources/libtool-2.5.4/build-aux/missing' automake-1.17

because the tarball's configure/Makefile.in were generated by automake 1.17 and
`am__api_version` is baked in at that value. The image has automake 1.18.1,
which installs `aclocal`/`aclocal-1.18` and nothing else. That is why declaring
`cmd:aclocal` in BUILD_PREREQUIRES changes nothing: the prerequisite resolves
fine (automake 1.18.1 does provide cmd:aclocal) and the build still calls a
binary that will never exist. Reading Makefile:1069 and Makefile:447 settles
this; guessing at recipe flags cannot.

## Why maintainer rules fire at all

The rules are the ordinary automake ones, e.g. Makefile:1069

    $(ACLOCAL_M4):  $(am__aclocal_m4_deps)
    	$(am__cd) $(srcdir) && $(ACLOCAL) $(ACLOCAL_AMFLAGS)

and Makefile:2390

    $(lt_Makefile_in): $(lt_Makefile_am) $(lt_aclocal_m4) $(lt_config_h_in)
    	$(AM_V_GEN)cd '$(srcdir)/$(ltdl_dir)' && $(AUTOMAKE) Makefile

A distribution tarball is supposed to build with no autotools installed, so
something must be making a prerequisite newer than its target. Two things are:

  1. **The recipe did it to itself.** `cp m4/libtool.m4 m4/libtool.m4.bak` has
     no -p, so the copy is stamped now; `mv m4/libtool.m4.bak m4/libtool.m4`
     puts that stamp back onto m4/libtool.m4, which the tarball shipped with the
     same date as aclocal.m4. m4/libtool.m4 is listed in `am__aclocal_m4_deps`
     (Makefile:104-113), so aclocal.m4 is now out of date and rule 1069 is live.

     That cp/mv exists only to undo what `./bootstrap --force` overwrites, and
     **bootstrap cannot run in this chroot at all**:

         ./bootstrap: line 4118: git: command not found
         bootstrap: warning: Consider installing git-merge-changelog from gnulib.
         bootstrap: running: git clone 'git://git.sv.gnu.org/gnulib' 'gnulib'
         ./bootstrap: line 4984: git: command not found

     There is no git in the image (haikuporter says so on its own first log
     line: "deactivating creation of source packages as 'git' is not
     available"), and even with git there is no network inside the chroot to
     clone gnulib from. bootstrap gives up after those two lines and does *not*
     fail the build, so the recipe's stated strategy -- regenerate with the
     newer automake so the stale aclocal-1.15/1.17 references go away --
     silently never happens. Meanwhile step 1 has guaranteed make will try.

  2. **The tarball's own timestamps are internally inconsistent.** In
     libtool-2.5.4.tar.gz the embedded libltdl subtree ships as

         libltdl/Makefile.am   2024-11-20 19:01
         libltdl/Makefile.in   2024-11-20 19:01
         libltdl/aclocal.m4    2024-11-20 19:41   <- newer than Makefile.in
         libltdl/config-h.in   2024-11-20 19:41   <- newer than Makefile.in

     so rule 2390 fires on *any* build of this tarball, independently of
     anything the recipe does, and dies on `automake-1.17`. Upstream never
     notices because ./bootstrap regenerates the lot first.

## The fix

Drop bootstrap and its cp/mv -- bootstrap is pure damage here, it accomplishes
nothing and the cleanup it requires is what arms rule 1069 -- then flatten every
timestamp in the pristine tree to one shared value. make only remakes a target
when a prerequisite is *strictly* newer, so one identical mtime across the whole
tree disables every automake/autoconf maintainer rule at once, everywhere,
without having to enumerate them. Everything make generates from then on is
stamped 2026 and is unambiguously newer than the flattened 2024 tree, so the
normal build rules are unaffected.

`touch -r <reference>` rather than a bare `touch`: this chroot's clock is not
trustworthy. In one run make reports `File 'Makefile' has modification time
66159 s in the future` and `Clock skew detected`, and configure's autobuild
timestamp (02:58:40Z) is 18h22m behind the guest's own `date` (21:21:10Z).
Taking the stamp from another file makes it independent of the clock. This is
also why the previously attempted plain pre-`touch` of the generated files could
not have worked even with a correct recipe -- it stamped them from a clock that
runs backwards.

The `chmod u+w` is required, not cosmetic: the tarball ships m4/ltversion.m4 and
libltdl/Makefile.am mode 444, and `touch` on those would fail and abort the
find.

Rejected alternative: shimming `aclocal-1.17`/`automake-1.17` onto automake
1.18.1. automake refuses a version mismatch, and even if forced it would
regenerate aclocal.m4, then configure, then every Makefile.in mid-build, at
which point `am__api_version` disagrees with the configure that already ran.
Not running the tools at all is both smaller and safer.

Applies to: input-source-packages/develop/sources/libtool-2.5.4-1/libtool-2.5.4.recipe
--- a/libtool-2.5.4.recipe
+++ b/libtool-2.5.4.recipe
@@ -67,12 +67,31 @@
 	export XZ=/bin/true
 	touch README-release

-	cp m4/libtool.m4 m4/libtool.m4.bak
-	./bootstrap --force
-	mv m4/libtool.m4.bak m4/libtool.m4
-		# bootstrap copies back the file from the system over the current ones.
-		# But we must run it, because libtool is shipped with old aclocal-1.15
-		# files and we have only a newer aclocal. So just restore the file.
+	# Deliberately no ./bootstrap: it needs git to clone gnulib, this image has
+	# no git and the chroot has no network, so it aborts after two "git: command
+	# not found" lines without failing the build and regenerates nothing. The
+	# generated files stay as automake 1.17 emitted them, and Makefile.in
+	# hardcodes ACLOCAL/AUTOMAKE = missing aclocal-1.17/automake-1.17 while only
+	# automake 1.18.1 is installed. The cp/mv of m4/libtool.m4 that used to
+	# guard bootstrap goes with it: cp without -p restamped m4/libtool.m4 to
+	# now, which made it newer than aclocal.m4 and armed the very maintainer
+	# rule that then died on the missing aclocal-1.17.
+	#
+	# Flatten every timestamp in the pristine tree to one shared value instead.
+	# make only remakes a target when a prerequisite is *strictly* newer, so one
+	# identical mtime disables every maintainer rule at once without having to
+	# enumerate them -- and they do need disabling beyond the cp/mv, because the
+	# 2.5.4 tarball's own timestamps are inconsistent: libltdl/aclocal.m4 and
+	# libltdl/config-h.in ship at 19:41 while libltdl/Makefile.in, which
+	# automake would regenerate from them, ships at 19:01. Anything make
+	# generates from here on is stamped 2026 and so is unambiguously newer than
+	# the flattened tree.
+	#
+	# The stamp comes from a reference file rather than a bare `touch` because
+	# this chroot's clock steps backwards -- make reports files "in the future"
+	# and warns about clock skew in the same run -- so a wall-clock stamp proves
+	# nothing. chmod first because the tarball ships m4/ltversion.m4 and
+	# libltdl/Makefile.am mode 444 and touch would fail on them.
+	touch -r configure.ac /tmp/lt-mtime-ref
+	find . -type f -exec chmod u+w {} +
+	find . -exec touch -r /tmp/lt-mtime-ref {} +
 	SED='sed' NM='nm' LD=ld runConfigure ./configure \
 		--with-gnu-ld \
 		--disable-static
